Their complex morphology, coupled with their distinctive function, sets the pancreatic D cells apart from other cells in the islets of Langerhans. They have long, neurite-like processes which allow them to make contact with other pancreatic endocrine cells at some distance from the cell body and create an extensive paracrine network. 
